  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 141 PM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThursday through Tuesday:\n\nHigher pressure is expected Thursday ahead of the next system.\nHigh temps will be in the upper 70s/lower 80s with lows in the\n60s. Winds will start out light and variable in the early morning\nhours, then will shift to be predominately from the west-southwest\nwhile continuing to be light. \n\nThe next cold front will move through the region on Friday with\nsporadic rain showers expected throughout the morning hours\n(20-40% chance). The main front is expected to pass over the CWA\nduring the afternoon/evening hours with increased PoPs (40-60% \nchance). Showers during this time will be more widespread along \nthe frontal boundary. There is a chance for thunderstorms with \nthis system as SBCAPE ranges from 1200 J/kg to 3000 J/kg with the \nhigher SBCAPE amounts near the IA/WI border which is where storms\ncould be stronger.\n\nAfter the passage of the cold front, high pressure will build \ninto the upper Midwest from Canada. Northwesterly winds will be \nthe predominate condition throughout the weekend which will \nadvect cooler and drier air from Canada as well. High temps will \ntop out in the mid to upper 70s inland and be slightly lower along\nthe shoreline with highs in the lower 70s. RH will be around 50% \neach day which will be a nice relief to conditions this past week.\n\nOn Monday as the high pressure continues to push eastward, another\nlow pressure system will begin to push into the region from\nSouthern Canada/NE MN. Winds will shift to be from the south,\nadvecting warmer and more moist air from the south which help set\nup the next chance for rain/storms. Highs expected in the uppers\n70s/low 80s inland and near the mid 70s along Lake Michigan.\n\nA cold front associated with the low pressure system will move\nacross the CWA on Tuesday. As of right now, timing is expected to\nbe in the late morning through early afternoon. Amount and\nintensity of any precipitation is uncertain at this point.\n\nSoergel/Patterson",
